Unavoidable legal pressures reduce AI interpretablity 2026-11-20

In recent weeks, there have been legal challenges against OpenAI relating to the
HuggingFace incident (TODO: add link to the cali ag complaint). What's interesting about the new challenges is that they
focus on the fact that OpenAI stored and reviewed reasoning traces from the
misbehaving agents and could have predicted misaligned behaviour from them:

> todo: add a quote from the doc supporting this

My conclusion is that challenges like this are inevitable and inexorably push
both users of AI systems and the labs themselves towards unobservable,
uninterpretable architectures. Note that this is kind of already happening -
OpenAI's Astra is widely thought to be at least partially recurrent (todo: neel
nanda's post on no cot leaps) 

I would prefer this were not true but I am having a hard time falsifying it, so
I'm taking the hit and updating my beliefs. I also don't think there's really
anything we can do about this, although I do have some suggestions towarsd the
end. The temporary dividend we received from chains of thought may be ending.

<!--more-->

## Step by step: which incentives are at play here?

I continue to be a fan of Charlie Munger: "Show me the incentives and I'll show
you the outcome" - the incentives at play are clear:

* Legislators have political incentives to attack the AI labs and firms using
  AI. This is supported by the general public independently of the merit of the
  challenges, but it happens to be the case that legal challenges based on
  stored reasoning traces probably do have merit. A judge seeing "I will now
  illegally hack HuggingFace" in a reasoning trace will reasonably conclude that
  this shows intent, and it could have been caught.

* Legal challenges create pressure of the form "if you can monitor, then you
  have to, and you have to act to stop illegal actions if you are warned". There
  are two ways to comply with this demand: monitoring and acting on warnings
  which increases costs and still incurs penalties if mistakes are made; or
  reduce interpretability of models to the point that effective monitoring is
  impossible - this reduces your costs and legal scrutiny.

* Firms using AI may in principle want to understand the reasoning behind
  answers. But the reasoning traces may be less than useful - todo: add marinade
  and disclaim citations from papers.

This kind of litigation creating incentives to reduce observability is already
well known. There is at least one paper about this from 1992 with some case
studies (todo link) but it's hard to avoid this conclusion with even a cursory
understanding of how humans work.


There are a few steps we need to go through to flesh out the incentives and
mechanisms.


